# dartman-movement-and-line-of-fire-rules

Dartman movement mechanics: Deflectors move like chess Rooks; the Dartman piece has unstoppable momentum and must travel until hitting the board edge or its own Deflector; crossing the opponent Dartman's line of fire is an instant loss.

## Procedures
- 1. Deflector moves (X1, X2, Y1, Y2): move any number of squares horizontally or vertically, exactly like a chess Rook. The 1x2 Deflector (X2/Y2) moves as a unit; both squares travel together. Deflectors cannot be rotated — the 1x2 piece always stays vertical.
- 2. Dartman moves (P1, P2): choose a horizontal or vertical direction. The Dartman travels as far as possible in that direction — it MUST stop only when it hits (a) the edge of the board, or (b) one of the moving player's own Deflector pieces. It cannot voluntarily stop mid-path and cannot use the opponent's Deflector pieces as a stopping point.
- 3. Before executing any Dartman move, check the line-of-fire rule: identify the full horizontal and vertical lines extending from the OPPONENT'S Dartman. If the move would place or pass P1/P2 onto any square in those lines — and there is no Deflector piece of EITHER player sitting between the two Dartmen along that line — the move is ILLEGAL and results in an INSTANT LOSS for the player who attempted it.
- 4. A Deflector piece of either color located between the two Dartmen along the line of fire acts as a blocker and makes a move through that line legal.
- 5. Players alternate turns; each turn one piece is moved once.

## Conventions
- The line-of-fire check must be applied BEFORE committing a Dartman move — attempting an illegal Dartman move forfeits the game immediately.
- Only the Dartman's OWN Deflectors can stop its momentum; the opponent's Deflectors are transparent as stopping points.
- Deflectors cannot jump over other pieces (same as chess Rook movement).

